'''Lichenoididae''' (sometimes misspelled ''Lichenoidae'') is a small extinct family of [[eocrinoid]] [[echinoderm]]s. It is best known from the type genus ''Lichenoides'', which unlike most other eocrinoids does not have a stalk.  At least one additional genus has been identified based on the presence of a "very short"<ref name=Zamora2010>{{harvnb|Zamora|2010|p=508}}</ref> or "rudimentary"<ref name=Smith2013>{{harvnb|Smith|Zamora|Javier Álvaro|2013|pp=5-6}}</ref> stalk, but has not yet been formally described.  The report of one new genus and species in Smith, Zamora, and Javier Álvaro (2013) does not mention the new genus and two species reported in Zamora (2010), so as of 2025 it is unclear how many additional genera and species have been found.


Lichenoidids with a very short stalk are thought to be more privitive than the un-stalked ''Lichenoides'',<ref name=Zamora2017>{{harvnb|Zamora|Deline|Javier Álvaro|Rahman|2017|p=482}}</ref> and have been found in Spain (Genestosa Member, Oville Formation (2 species); Murero Formation, Iberian Chain) and Morocco (Tarhoucht Member, Jbel Wawrmast Formation).

